*Description of problem:*

{noformat}When a cluster is deployed using the User Provisioned DNS capabilities, 
the CoreDNS configuration file for the static pods this capability deploys
includes the IP of the machine itself as the first forwarding server leading to a recursive loop.

Here is an example of the forward configuration from a cluster I 
recently deployed. NODE_IP is just a stand in for the IP of the node:

....
forward . [NODE_IP] 168.63.129.16 {
        policy sequential
        force_tcp
    }
.....
{noformat}

{noformat}{noformat}

{noformat}{noformat}

*Version-Release number of selected component (if applicable):*

{noformat}{noformat}

*How reproducible:*

{noformat}It happens every time you deploy a cluster in Azure(likely happens on other
cloud providers but I have not tested that) using the userProvisionedDNS feature{noformat}

*Steps to Reproduce:*

{noformat}1. Create an install-config.yaml to deploy a cluster in Azure with
userProvisionedDNS:·Enabled
2. Provision the cluster 
3. Once the cluster is up query for an external DNS hostname from a pod
4. Look at the logs of the static pods and notice all the timeouts where 
the client and server is the node itself{noformat}

*Actual results:*

{noformat}A bunch of i/o errors related to the recursive dns look ups and slowness
when resolving external hostnames{noformat}

*Expected results:*

{noformat}Queries for external hostnames should be forwarded to the VPC DNS Resolver
right away{noformat}

*Additional info:*

{noformat}{noformat}

